@startuml Composite-Equipment-Before
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
title GoF Equipment Example \nBefore Composite
note as Caveat #FFE0B2
  <b>Read this first</b>

  GoF gives a "before" design in the graphics
  Motivation (p. 163) but NOT for the equipment
  Sample Code (pp. 170–173).

  This diagram is a reconstruction: what the
  same example looks like if the pattern is
  never applied. It is here for the contrast
  with Composite-Equipment.png, not as
  something the book says.
end note

package "the simple equipment — no common type" {
  class FloppyDisk {
    - watts : int
    - listPrice : Currency
    + power() : int
    + netPrice() : Currency
    + discountPrice() : Currency
  }

  class Card {
    - watts : int
    - listPrice : Currency
    + power() : int
    + netPrice() : Currency
    + discountPrice() : Currency
  }
}

package "the assemblies — each one a special case" {
  class Bus {
    - watts : int
    - listPrice : Currency
    - cards : List<Card>
    + add(c : Card) : void
    + power() : int
    + netPrice() : Currency
    + discountPrice() : Currency
  }
  note bottom of Bus
    Three roll-up methods,
    each looping over `cards`.
  end note

  class Chassis {
    - watts : int
    - listPrice : Currency
    - buses : List<Bus>
    - floppyDisks : List<FloppyDisk>
    + add(b : Bus) : void
    + add(f : FloppyDisk) : void
    + power() : int
    + netPrice() : Currency
    + discountPrice() : Currency
  }
  note bottom of Chassis
    Three MORE roll-up methods,
    each looping over BOTH of its
    collections.

    Forget one loop and the price
    is silently too low.
  end note

  class Cabinet {
    - listPrice : Currency
    - chassis : List<Chassis>
    + add(c : Chassis) : void
    + power() : int
    + netPrice() : Currency
    + discountPrice() : Currency
  }
  note bottom of Cabinet
    Three MORE.

    9 near-identical aggregating
    methods across 3 containers,
    for 3 questions.
  end note
}

class InventoryReport {
  + printTree(item : Object) : void
  + totalPrice(item : Object) : Currency
}
note right of InventoryReport
  The client

  There is no type meaning
  "a piece of equipment", so a
  report that walks an assembly
  must take Object and branch:

    if (o instanceof Cabinet) ...
    else if (o instanceof Chassis) ...
    else if (o instanceof Bus) ...
    else if (o instanceof Card) ...
    else if (o instanceof FloppyDisk) ...

  Five branches, and a new
  equipment type makes six.
end note

' four collections across three containers — the field names are in the boxes
Cabinet *--> "*" Chassis
Chassis *--> "*" Bus
Chassis *--> "*" FloppyDisk
Bus *--> "*" Card

InventoryReport ..> Cabinet : instanceof
InventoryReport ..> Card : instanceof

note as Costs
  <b>What this design costs</b>
  • No common type → clients take Object and branch.
  • 9 near-identical roll-up methods, one set per container.
  • 4 child collections, each with its own add() overload.
  • A new equipment type means editing every container
    that may hold it, plus every client.

  <b>...and the one thing it genuinely buys</b>
  The containment rules are enforced by the COMPILER:
  bus.add(cabinet) does not compile. A Bus holds only
  Cards; a Chassis holds only Buses and FloppyDisks.

  Composite gives that up. Equipment.add(Equipment)
  accepts anything, so "a Bus may only hold Cards"
  becomes a run-time check or no check at all —
  exactly GoF's fourth consequence (p. 166):
  <i>"you cannot rely on the type system to enforce</i>
  <i>those constraints for you."</i>

  See Composite-Equipment.png.
end note

@enduml
